  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Turkiye and Syria Earthquake</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">LRC SAR mission EQ Turkey and Syria</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On February 6th 2023, several earthquakes hit southern Turkey and northern Syria, with the strongest one measuring 7.8 on the Richter scale. The epicenter was in Gaziantep, near the border of Turkey and Syria. Over 1,206 aftershocks have been recorded since then. The earthquake caused significant damage to north, central, south, and coastal areas of Syria, with Aleppo, Hama, Idleb, and Lattakia Governorates being hit the hardest. The disaster has caused immense damage to infrastructure and homes, leaving many people without necessities such as food, water, and shelter, as well as requiring medical and psychosocial assistance. As of February 12th, at least 5,791 people have died and 10,041 have been injured in Syria, with most of the deaths and injuries occurring in northwest Syria. By the 7th of March, reports identified that the overall death toll has surpassed 50,000 and 2.2 million people have been displaced, including 1.9 million who have relocated outside of the earthquake-impacted areas.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">People living in the affected areas</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The LRC has deployed two teams to support rescue and evacuations operations.</narrative>
  </description>
